import SGPT.Checks.Comparison.GfImSkoda
import SGPT.Checks.Comparison.BJAsmVarphian
import SGPT.NAPotential.GAGAMult2SectionIdeal
import SGPT.Foundations.Analysis.Pending.IEnvelopeStrongOpenness

/-!
# Analytifications of potentials of finitely many algebraic sections

Infrastructure for `lma:gfinimage` (`chapter_NApotential.tex`, *The comparison theorem*): the
proof writes `A_{τ_i} = m⁻¹ max_{j ∈ I_{τ_i}} log |s_j|²_{h^m}` and uses
`A_{τ_i}^An(v) = m⁻¹ max_j log |s_j|(v) = -m⁻¹ min_j v(s_j)` at every `v ∈ X^An`.

Let `X` be smooth projective over `ℂ` of relative dimension `n > 0`, `(M, h_M)` a metrized
algebraic line bundle, `t_k ∈ H⁰(X, M^N)` finitely many nonzero sections and
`Φ = max_k log |t_k|²_{h_M^N}` (`sectionsMaxLog`), and let `𝔟 = ∑_k (t_k)` be the ideal sheaf
generated by the sections (`sectionsIdeal`). Then for every `K > 0` and every `v ∈ X^An`
(`potentialAn_sectionsMaxLog`):

`(K⁻¹ Φ)^An(v) = -K⁻¹ v(𝔟) = -K⁻¹ min_k v(t_k)`.

Proof: near every point, `Φ = log ∑_k |g_k|² + R` with `g_k` the frame coefficients of the `t_k`
(generators of `𝔟^an`) and `R` bounded (`exists_local_presentation`). The easy inclusion
`𝔟^j ⊆ alg 𝓘(jΦ)` (`pow_le_algebraization`) follows from `GfIm.prod_mem_multiplierIdeal`, and
Skoda's theorem gives `alg 𝓘((j + n)Φ) ⊆ 𝔟^j` (`algebraization_le_pow`), using GAGA for ideal
sheaves (`algebraization_analytificationIdeal`). Hence
`j v(𝔟) ≤ v(𝓘((j+n)Φ)) ≤ (j + n) v(𝔟)` and the limit defining `(K⁻¹Φ)^An` is `K⁻¹ v(𝔟)`.
No divisorial valuation, resolution or Briançon–Skoda/integral-closure argument is used.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false
set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false

noncomputable section

namespace SGPT.NAPotential.BJ.GfIm

open Set Filter
